
Functions

    What:
        A function is a self‑contained block of code that performs a specific task.
        It is defined once and can be called (invoked) multiple times.

    Why:
        Reusability     – Write once, use many times.
        Modularity      – Break a large program into smaller, manageable parts.
        Abstraction     – Hide internal implementation details; expose only needed interface.
        Maintainability – Easier to debug, update, and extend.

    How:
        Three steps are involved:
        1. Function Declaration (prototype)
        2. Function Definition
        3. Function Call

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    1. Function Declaration (Prototype)
        • Tells the compiler about the function’s name, return type, and parameters.
        • It is mandatory if the function definition appears AFTER the function call.
        • Even when the definition comes before the call, declaring prototypes at the top
          is considered good programming practice.

        Syntax:
            return_type function_name(parameter_list);

        Example:
            void class_count(int count);

        • If no declaration is given before the call, the compiler may raise an
          "implicit declaration" error or warning.

    2. Function Definition
        • Contains the actual body of the function – the instructions to be executed.
        • Must match the prototype exactly (return type, name, parameters).

        Syntax:
            return_type function_name(parameters)
            {
                // statements
            }

        Example:
            void class_count(int count)
            {
                count = 60;
                printf("%d\n", count);
            }

    3. Function Call
        • Transfers control to the function along with actual arguments (if any).
        • The function executes its code and, if not void, returns a value.

        Syntax:
            function_name(arguments);

        Example:
            class_count(65);   // call with integer argument

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Categories Based on Return Value

        With return value:
            • The function computes a result and sends it back using the `return` keyword.
            • Return type must be specified (int, float, double, char*, etc.).

        Without return value (void):
            • The function performs a task but does not return any value.
            • Declared with `void` return type.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Parameter Passing Mechanisms

        Pass by Value:
            • A copy of the argument is passed to the function.
            • Changes made inside the function do NOT affect the original variable.

            Example:
                #include <stdio.h>
                void class_count(int);
                void class_count(int count)
                {
                    count = 60;
                    printf("%d\n", count);    // 60
                }
                int main()
                {
                    int count = 65;
                    class_count(count);       // pass by value
                    printf("%d", count);      // 65 (unchanged)
                    return 0;
                }

        Pass by Reference (using pointers):
            • The address of the variable is passed.
            • The function can modify the original variable directly.

            Example:
                #include <stdio.h>
                void class_count(int*);
                void class_count(int *count)
                {
                    *count = 60;
                    printf("%d\n", *count);   // 60
                }
                int main()
                {
                    int count = 65;
                    class_count(&count);      // pass by reference
                    printf("%d", count);      // 60 (changed)
                    return 0;
                }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Presence of Parameters

        With Parameter:
            • The function receives one or more values from the caller.
            • Used when the function needs external data to operate.

            Example:
                #include <stdio.h>
                void class_count(int);
                void class_count(int count)
                {
                    printf("%d\n", count);
                }
                int main()
                {
                    int count = 65;
                    class_count(count);       // argument passed
                    return 0;
                }

        Without Parameter:
            • The function takes no arguments.
            • It performs a fixed task that does not depend on external input.

            Example:
                #include <stdio.h>
                void greetings();
                void greetings()
                {
                    printf("Good Morning\n");
                }
                int main()
                {
                    greetings();              // no argument
                    return 0;
                }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Order of Definition and Declaration

        • Definition before main() → Declaration is optional.
            #include <stdio.h>
            void greetings()
            {
                printf("Good Morning\n");
            }
            int main()
            {
                greetings();
                return 0;
            }

        • Definition after main() → Declaration is mandatory.
            #include <stdio.h>
            void greetings();   // declaration
            int main()
            {
                greetings();
                return 0;
            }
            void greetings()
            {
                printf("Good Morning\n");
            }

        • Missing declaration when definition is after main() causes:
            "implicit declaration of function" error.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Summary of Function Types with Examples

        1. Without return, without parameter
           void greet() { printf("Hello"); }

        2. Without return, with parameter
           void display(int n) { printf("%d", n); }

        3. With return, without parameter
           int giveMeFive() { return 5; }

        4. With return, with parameter
           int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }

        5. Pass by reference (modifies original)
           void swap(int *x, int *y) { int t = *x; *x = *y; *y = t; }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Remember:
        • Function Declaration  – Informs the compiler.
        • Function Definition   – Implements the logic.
        • Function Call         – Executes the function.
        • Parameters are optional.
        • Return type can be void or any valid C data type.
        • Use pass‑by‑reference when the function must modify the original argument.


